<!-- DUAL RENDERING MODEL -->
<section id="rendering" class="prose-section">
  <div class="site-container">
    <p class="prose-eyebrow prose-eyebrow-native">Architecture &middot; Dual rendering</p>
    <h2 class="prose-h2">Choose native look-and-feel or a GPU canvas &mdash; per app or per screen</h2>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      Other cross-platform frameworks lock you into one side of the trade-off: Flutter and Compose Multiplatform draw their own
      widget copies on a canvas; React Native wraps platform views behind a JavaScript engine. Osprey Native runs both rendering
      paths over the exact same typed store, signals, and shared-memory bridge.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-grid-2">
      <article class="prose-card prose-card-native">
        <span class="badge badge-native">&#9635; Native Controls Mode</span>
        <h3 class="prose-h3">Authentic OS widgets everywhere</h3>
        <p class="prose-body">
          Write semantic <code>Node&lt;A&gt;</code> trees (<code>Split</code>, <code>Screen</code>, <code>Rows</code>, <code>Button</code>, <code>Field</code>, <code>Toggle</code>, <code>Sheet</code>).
          Each host leases the frame in place and draws real platform widgets:
        </p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li><strong>macOS &amp; iOS:</strong> SwiftUI stacks, toolbars, native search fields, and SF Symbols.</li>
          <li><strong>Windows:</strong> WinUI from the Windows App SDK, Segoe Fluent Icons, sandboxed MSIX AppContainer.</li>
          <li><strong>Android:</strong> JNI direct byte buffers read by Kotlin into Android's own views.</li>
          <li><strong>Web &amp; VS Code:</strong> WebAssembly module reading the exact same row offsets via <code>DataView</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-card prose-card-canvas">
        <span class="badge badge-canvas">&#9670; FerroUI / GPUI Canvas Mode</span>
        <h3 class="prose-h3">Pixel-identical GPU rendering like Flutter</h3>
        <p class="prose-body">
          Need custom audio timelines, interactive boards, vector painting, or bespoke skins that look identical on every OS?
          Target the <strong>FerroUI</strong> (or GPUI) GPU-accelerated host using the same Osprey library:
        </p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li><strong>Same AOT binary:</strong> your compiled C-ABI archive runs unchanged on FerroUI's drawing context.</li>
          <li><strong>High-throughput scenes:</strong> Flock streams 5,000 live sprites in ~7 ms per frame, <code>update</code> included.</li>
          <li><strong>UI machines &amp; skins:</strong> declare parallel state machines and atlas skins in validated documents with built-in hit testing.</li>
          <li><strong>No JavaScript engine:</strong> native desktop and mobile builds ship zero JS runtime and zero JIT warm-up.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SIGNALS FROM THE GROUND UP -->
<section id="signals" class="prose-section">
  <div class="site-container">
    <p class="prose-eyebrow prose-eyebrow-verified">Reactivity &middot; Compiler-derived</p>
    <h2 class="prose-h2">Signals from the ground up &mdash; zero dependency arrays</h2>
    <p class="prose-lead">
      Reactivity is not a library bolted onto a widget tree. Osprey's compiler tracks which <code>Signal&lt;T&gt;</code> slices
      each keyed region reads, refreshing only the regions whose inputs changed and retaining everything else.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-grid-3">
      <article class="prose-card prose-card-verified">
        <h3 class="prose-h3">1. Typed Slice Signals</h3>
        <p class="prose-body">
          Every state slice has its own record type and identity (<code>Signal&lt;Query&gt;</code>, <code>Signal&lt;Selection&gt;</code>).
          Reads happen through typed effects; writes happen only in pure <code>update</code> functions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-card prose-card-verified">
        <h3 class="prose-h3">2. Keyed Regions</h3>
        <p class="prose-body">
          <code>Keyed::region</code> snapshots each region's compiler-derived dependencies. When one slice updates, unchanged
          regions keep their revision, focus, scroll anchor, and IME state intact.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-card prose-card-verified">
        <h3 class="prose-h3">3. Effects at the Edges</h3>
        <p class="prose-body">
          Files, networking, host-held slices (<code>Slices.fetch</code>), and frame output (<code>ViewStream</code>) are typed
          algebraic effects. Production handlers talk to the OS; test handlers swap in deterministic fixtures.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

