    <h3 class="prose-h3" style="margin-block-start: var(--space-5);">Where Osprey Native pulls ahead of Compose Multiplatform (CMP)</h3>
    <div class="prose-grid-3" style="margin-block-start: var(--space-2);">
      <article class="prose-card prose-card-native">
        <span class="badge badge-native">&#9635; Beyond Skia-Only UI</span>
        <h4 class="prose-h3">True native controls on iOS, macOS &amp; Windows</h4>
        <p class="prose-body">
          CMP draws shared UI on Skia and hosts desktop in a JVM/Swing shell. Osprey Native renders real SwiftUI on Apple
          and real WinUI on Windows &mdash; and lets you switch to FerroUI's GPU canvas whenever you want pixel-identical scenes.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-card prose-card-canvas">
        <span class="badge badge-canvas">&#9670; No JVM &middot; No Kotlin Lock-In</span>
        <h4 class="prose-h3">AOT native everywhere, in your flavor</h4>
        <p class="prose-body">
          No JVM startup cost on desktop, no JSON bridge, and no single-language mandate. Teams write in Default Osprey, ML,
          Python, or TypeScript flavor (with Dart coming soon) over one lease-checked shared-memory ABI.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-card prose-card-verified">
        <span class="badge badge-verified">&#10003; Built for AI Agents</span>
        <h4 class="prose-h3">Structural accessibility + effect-mocked tests</h4>
        <p class="prose-body">
          UI machines make roles and live labels structural rather than optional modifiers, so screen readers, headless
          <code>@osprey/uitest</code> runs, and AI agents share the exact same verified interaction surface.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

